00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:03,680
Rather a hypothesis.

268
00:10:03,680 --> 00:10:05,160
Right, now there is an experiment

269
00:10:05,160 --> 00:10:07,340
that fits very nicely with what I'm saying.

270
00:10:07,340 --> 00:10:09,800
So Gordon Tullock, the great Tullock,

271
00:10:09,800 --> 00:10:10,720
who has passed from us,

272
00:10:10,720 --> 00:10:12,900
but his spirit is always with us.

273
00:10:12,900 --> 00:10:15,280
And every time the Nobel Prize is given out,

274
00:10:15,280 --> 00:10:17,240
it is the duty of every GMU grad

275
00:10:17,240 --> 00:10:20,220
to curse the Nobel Committee in your heart

276
00:10:20,220 --> 00:10:22,480
for not having given him the prize.

277
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:24,200
He totally deserved it.

278
00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:26,240
All right, but in any case, the great Tullock.

279
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:28,600
He, as far as I know, he only did one economic experiment

280
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:29,440
his entire career,

281
00:10:29,440 --> 00:10:31,760
and it was one of the most brilliant experiments ever done,

282
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:33,440
and was super cheap and easy to do,

283
00:10:33,440 --> 00:10:36,960
but he alone saw some low-hanging fruit.

284
00:10:36,960 --> 00:10:38,040
So here's his experiment.

285
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:40,560
He repeated the Prisoner of Selma experiment

286
00:10:40,560 --> 00:10:42,800
that's been done so many times,

287
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:44,320
but with one twist.

288
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:46,320
You can change your partner if you want.

289
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:48,440
That's the twist.

290
00:10:48,440 --> 00:10:50,600
So normally in the Prisoner of Selma experiment,

291
00:10:50,600 --> 00:10:51,800
you get paired up with a person,

292
00:10:51,800 --> 00:10:52,760
and then you can assign,

293
00:10:52,760 --> 00:10:54,320
and then you may work with them,

294
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:56,160
or you make it randomly reassigned.

295
00:10:56,160 --> 00:10:57,960
Tullock said, no, you're not stuck with them.

296
00:10:57,960 --> 00:10:59,800
You're not randomly reassigned.

297
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:02,400
You are reassigned by choice.

298
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:03,640
So you can fire your partner

299
00:11:03,640 --> 00:11:06,520
and find another partner anytime you want.

300
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:08,360
All right, and the result of the experiment,

301
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:09,200
as you might guess,

302
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:11,600
was that there was a very high cooperation,

303
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:15,000
because the person who was thinking of defecting

304
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:16,240
in the Prisoner of Selma experiment,

305
00:11:16,240 --> 00:11:18,840
when that happens, quickly becomes known,

306
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:20,240
and then your partner,

307
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:21,640
the first step is,

308
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:22,480
what's the first step?

309
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:23,640
Really obvious one.

310
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:25,120
You stay, you betray your partner,

311
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:26,400
and then what happens?

312
00:11:27,560 --> 00:11:28,760
Your partner dumps you.

313
00:11:28,760 --> 00:11:30,720
All right, but then the really scary part

314
00:11:30,720 --> 00:11:32,480
of the second is the next thing.

315
00:11:32,480 --> 00:11:34,560
All right, now you gotta find a new partner.

316
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:36,240
Who will have you now?

317
00:11:37,640 --> 00:11:38,680
Nobody.

318
00:11:38,680 --> 00:11:40,360
Well, I think in the experiment,

319
00:11:40,360 --> 00:11:41,200
you have to have a partner.

320
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:42,160
You have to be part of the experiment,

321
00:11:42,160 --> 00:11:44,040
but it'll be another traitor,

322
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:45,440
another betrayer.

323
00:11:45,440 --> 00:11:46,720
So then you just get stuck

324
00:11:46,720 --> 00:11:48,280
into this pool of other people

325
00:11:48,280 --> 00:11:50,480
that nobody else wants to deal with.

326
00:11:50,480 --> 00:11:53,240
So it's like kind of being in prison.

327
00:11:53,240 --> 00:11:55,440
Your only friends are people who kill people,

328
00:11:55,440 --> 00:11:57,880
rob people, sell, it's scary, right?

329
00:11:57,880 --> 00:11:59,480
I don't wanna be with those people.

330
00:11:59,480 --> 00:12:00,920
Well, then don't get in there.

331
00:12:00,920 --> 00:12:03,120
Then you won't have to deal with these problems.

332
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:03,960
All right, so anyway,

333
00:12:03,960 --> 00:12:05,880
that was the idea of the talk experiment,

334
00:12:05,880 --> 00:12:07,080
and I think you got something close

335
00:12:07,080 --> 00:12:08,320
to full cooperation,

336
00:12:08,320 --> 00:12:11,280
where people were very quickly able to see,

337
00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:13,280
wait a second, if I betray you,

338
00:12:13,280 --> 00:12:15,560
not only will you not like me anymore,

339
00:12:15,560 --> 00:12:16,840
but I will be damaged goods.

340
00:12:16,840 --> 00:12:18,480
No one will like me anymore,

341
00:12:18,480 --> 00:12:21,720
and then my only partner will be scum like me.

342
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:23,840
I don't wanna be partnered with scum like me.

343
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:26,240
So that's what he got.

344
00:12:26,240 --> 00:12:29,400
All right, questions, questions.

345
00:12:31,080 --> 00:12:34,560
So now let's move on to monopoly and contestability.

346
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:35,840
All right, so I assume everyone here

347
00:12:35,840 --> 00:12:38,720
has seen the standard monopoly model a bunch of times.

348
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:41,640
So standard monopoly model,

349
00:12:41,640 --> 00:12:43,720
where you've got your marginal cost,

350
00:12:43,720 --> 00:12:45,600
you've got your demand,

351
00:12:45,600 --> 00:12:48,680
and then of course you have your marginal revenue curve,

352
00:12:48,680 --> 00:12:50,640
and then you monopolize that marginal revenue

353
00:12:50,640 --> 00:12:52,080
for a marginal cost,

354
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:54,960
providing these monopoly profits,

355
00:12:54,960 --> 00:12:57,640
and creating these deadweight losses here.

356
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:02,920
So everyone's seen this model a bunch of times, right?

357
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:07,920
Okay, all right, so monopolist maximizes PQ minus TC,

358
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:10,640
says marginal revenue plus marginal cost.

359
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:14,160
All right, so does this make sense in game theoretic terms?

360
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:16,640
All right, so certainly this is an equilibrium.

361
00:13:16,640 --> 00:13:18,520
This is one equilibrium,

362
00:13:18,520 --> 00:13:21,840
but we can imagine all kinds of other equilibria

363
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:24,680
in this monopoly game.

364
00:13:24,680 --> 00:13:27,480
So we can imagine one where consumers refuse to buy

365
00:13:27,480 --> 00:13:29,840
at any price higher than marginal cost,

366
00:13:29,840 --> 00:13:31,360
and if the consumers won't buy anything

367
00:13:31,360 --> 00:13:32,760
at a price higher than marginal cost,

368
00:13:32,760 --> 00:13:34,360
what is the monopolist gonna do?

369
00:13:35,960 --> 00:13:36,800
I'll set price.

370
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:38,440
Yes, we'll set price equal to marginal cost.

371
00:13:38,440 --> 00:13:41,080
So I got nothing to lose then.

372
00:13:41,080 --> 00:13:41,920
Yes.

373
00:13:41,920 --> 00:13:44,480
My question is, is it possible for the consumer

374
00:13:44,480 --> 00:13:48,400
to refuse to buy if the monopolist's set P

375
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:50,680
is greater than a minus?

376
00:13:50,680 --> 00:13:54,000
Well, so we can certainly imagine an equilibrium

377
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,040
where this is an equilibrium.

378
00:13:56,040 --> 00:13:57,320
So if you just think of this,

379
00:13:57,320 --> 00:13:59,240
what I described is a Nash equilibrium, right?

380
00:13:59,240 --> 00:14:02,520
Because if consumers will not buy anything

381
00:14:02,520 --> 00:14:04,640
if the price is greater than marginal cost,

382
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:08,720
then if the monopolist says,

383
00:14:08,720 --> 00:14:10,640
charging price equal to marginal cost,

384
00:14:10,640 --> 00:14:12,520
if consumers decide they're willing to pay more,

385
00:14:12,520 --> 00:14:14,220
does that give them a better deal?

386
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:17,200
Yes, so given that they're already getting

387
00:14:17,200 --> 00:14:18,680
at a marginal cost, if they decide

388
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:19,680
that they're willing to pay more,

389
00:14:19,680 --> 00:14:21,380
does that give them a better deal?

390
00:14:22,440 --> 00:14:23,280
No.

391
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:25,520
If the monopolist,

392
00:14:25,520 --> 00:14:27,080
holding constant consumer behavior,

393
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:28,120
decides to raise his price,

394
00:14:28,120 --> 00:14:30,280
does that get the monopolist anything extra?

395
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:36,360
No, because, so the equilibrium is consumers

396
00:14:36,680 --> 00:14:38,860
only don't buy, so yeah.

397
00:14:39,880 --> 00:14:44,720
Don't buy if price is greater than marginal cost,

398
00:14:44,720 --> 00:14:47,980
and monopolist does, price equal to marginal cost.

399
00:14:47,980 --> 00:14:50,440
So from the monopolist's point of view,

400
00:14:50,440 --> 00:14:52,200
if he recharges more,

401
00:14:52,200 --> 00:14:55,320
then he's actually going to earn nothing, right?

402
00:14:55,320 --> 00:14:56,840
And from the consumer's point of view,

403
00:14:56,840 --> 00:14:58,580
if they suddenly become willing to pay more,

404
00:14:58,580 --> 00:15:00,480
they still get a marginal cost,

405
00:15:00,480 --> 00:15:04,440
so this is also a Nash equilibrium, okay?

406
00:15:04,440 --> 00:15:06,480
And of course, we can have all kinds of equilibria

407
00:15:06,480 --> 00:15:08,680
in between these extremes, right?

408
00:15:08,680 --> 00:15:12,120
So we can have, consumers don't buy

409
00:15:12,120 --> 00:15:15,640
if price is greater than marginal cost plus a penny,

410
00:15:15,640 --> 00:15:17,960
right, and that could be another equilibrium.

411
00:15:17,960 --> 00:15:22,200
All right, now, but to get to your deeper question,

412
00:15:22,200 --> 00:15:23,600
to your deeper question,

413
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:27,480
what do we need to add to the game theory

414
00:15:27,480 --> 00:15:29,000
in order to get a result,

415
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:30,800
in order to get the standard result?

416
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:32,120
Like, why is this equilibrium,

417
00:15:32,120 --> 00:15:34,680
why does it make more sense than these other ones,

418
00:15:34,680 --> 00:15:37,040
which are still perfectly good Nash equilibria?

419
00:15:38,040 --> 00:15:38,880
Yes?

420
00:15:38,880 --> 00:15:41,520
Yeah, yeah, so you might do sub-game perfection,

421
00:15:41,520 --> 00:15:43,400
so yeah, so the monopolist goes

422
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:44,960
and charges a higher price,

423
00:15:44,960 --> 00:15:46,440
and then the consumers are there,

424
00:15:46,440 --> 00:15:47,280
what are they gonna do?

425
00:15:47,280 --> 00:15:49,760
No, I'm just gonna sit here starving.

426
00:15:49,760 --> 00:15:51,360
That doesn't seem very smart.

427
00:15:51,360 --> 00:15:53,880
So, yes, this threat to refuse to buy

428
00:15:53,880 --> 00:15:55,200
the price is greater than marginal cost

429
00:15:55,200 --> 00:15:57,720
sounds like a herdedly threat, right?

430
00:15:57,720 --> 00:15:58,720
And then on top of it, of course,

431
00:15:58,720 --> 00:16:00,400
the monopolist might build up a reputation,

432
00:16:00,400 --> 00:16:02,720
saying, look, I don't respond to this kind of stuff,

433
00:16:02,720 --> 00:16:04,260
and in the real world, it's a lot easier

434
00:16:04,260 --> 00:16:06,200
for one firm to build a reputation

435
00:16:06,200 --> 00:16:09,080
than for a million consumers, right?

436
00:16:09,080 --> 00:16:12,360
Because, how is one consumer gonna say,

437
00:16:12,360 --> 00:16:13,200
you know what I'm gonna do?

438
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:15,280
I'm gonna teach this monopolist a lesson.

439
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:16,160
All right, you can try,

440
00:16:16,160 --> 00:16:18,600
and if you pay close attention,

441
00:16:18,600 --> 00:16:19,960
in the real world, people do try

442
00:16:19,960 --> 00:16:21,840
this kind of thing sometimes.

443
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:24,760
So, I remember after the Exxon,

444
00:16:24,760 --> 00:16:26,680
oh, the Valdez oil spill,

445
00:16:26,680 --> 00:16:30,040
my brother refused to buy gas from Exxon for a while.

446
00:16:30,040 --> 00:16:31,120
I'm gonna show them.

447
00:16:32,200 --> 00:16:34,960
So sometimes people do do things like this,

448
00:16:34,960 --> 00:16:38,440
but normally, of course, this sounds like

449
00:16:38,440 --> 00:16:41,440
wishful thinking that you're gonna make a difference, right?

450
00:16:41,440 --> 00:16:44,560
Okay, anyway, so as far as,

451
00:16:44,560 --> 00:16:46,720
so the standard textbook story,

452
00:16:46,720 --> 00:16:48,040
when you think about it,

453
00:16:48,040 --> 00:16:50,680
seems pretty good as far as it goes, right?

454
00:16:50,680 --> 00:16:54,080
But there is an obvious problem with the textbook story,

455
00:16:54,080 --> 00:16:56,580
and that is that it ignores potential competition.

456
00:17:01,720 --> 00:17:03,400
All right, potential competition.

457
00:17:03,400 --> 00:17:04,720
All right, the idea that just because

458
00:17:04,720 --> 00:17:06,320
there's only one firm now,

459
00:17:06,320 --> 00:17:08,120
doesn't mean that there couldn't be other firms

460
00:17:08,120 --> 00:17:10,120
if circumstances changed.

461
00:17:10,120 --> 00:17:12,120
Of course, if there is,

462
00:17:12,120 --> 00:17:13,960
if this is a government-created monopoly,

463
00:17:13,960 --> 00:17:15,640
or anyone who competes goes to jail,

464
00:17:15,640 --> 00:17:17,200
then the simple model is great.

465
00:17:18,520 --> 00:17:20,240
So, if it is illegal to compete,

466
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:21,520
then this is a fine model.

467
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:26,940
But if it just happens to be only one firm around,

468
00:17:27,960 --> 00:17:30,480
then the question is,

469
00:17:30,480 --> 00:17:32,560
well, how do you get to be the only firm?

470
00:17:32,560 --> 00:17:35,640
And how do you make sure you remain the only firm?

471
00:17:35,640 --> 00:17:38,800
So, it sure doesn't seem like Amazon is acting this way,

472
00:17:38,800 --> 00:17:41,680
even though when Amazon started charging sales tax

473
00:17:41,680 --> 00:17:43,720
in Virginia, I did ask my students,

474
00:17:43,720 --> 00:17:47,060
so what is the second best firm like Amazon?

475
00:17:48,240 --> 00:17:50,120
And people told me something like,

476
00:17:50,120 --> 00:17:51,920
Recruitment or something like that.

477
00:17:53,640 --> 00:17:55,080
Started down, so it was something,

478
00:17:55,080 --> 00:17:56,280
it was some Japanese name,

479
00:17:56,280 --> 00:17:59,400
and I sort of looked at it, it didn't seem very good.

480
00:17:59,400 --> 00:18:02,600
So anyway, if Amazon ceased to exist,

481
00:18:02,600 --> 00:18:04,840
where would you buy your stuff from instead?

482
00:18:05,960 --> 00:18:06,920
I'll leave it at Walmart.

483
00:18:06,920 --> 00:18:08,000
Alibaba?

484
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,360
Well, Walmart, hey, it might actually go

485
00:18:10,360 --> 00:18:11,960
to a brick and mortar store,

486
00:18:11,960 --> 00:18:14,600
I guess you could do Walmart.com, that sounds better.

487
00:18:16,600 --> 00:18:18,000
All right, but in any case,

488
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,160
so on the surface it seems like Amazon

489
00:18:20,160 --> 00:18:23,040
really has a fantastic position.

490
00:18:23,040 --> 00:18:25,840
People can't even think of what they would do without it,

491
00:18:25,840 --> 00:18:30,280
and yet, the pricing on Amazon seems very low,

492
00:18:30,280 --> 00:18:32,040
for the most part.

493
00:18:32,040 --> 00:18:34,120
In fact, it's like, how can it be this low?

494
00:18:34,120 --> 00:18:36,360
It's so great, it's fantastic.

495
00:18:37,760 --> 00:18:40,400
So, it did take me a few years

496
00:18:40,400 --> 00:18:41,960
before I really internalized,

497
00:18:41,960 --> 00:18:43,480
always looking on Amazon for things,

498
00:18:43,480 --> 00:18:45,560
but finally I've achieved this.

499
00:18:45,560 --> 00:18:46,800
You may have grown up in a world

500
00:18:46,800 --> 00:18:48,920
where you don't even know the names of other places

501
00:18:48,920 --> 00:18:50,480
that you might buy things.

502
00:18:50,480 --> 00:18:53,800
Yeah, I think I, 15 years ago I needed a new backpack,

503
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:55,840
and I went around to different stores,

504
00:18:55,840 --> 00:18:57,440
and then I said, wait, why did I do that?

505
00:18:57,440 --> 00:19:01,040
Why didn't I just go to Amazon and try to get backpacks?

506
00:19:01,040 --> 00:19:03,960
I knew that would be better, but anyway.

507
00:19:03,960 --> 00:19:05,440
All right, so the main problem of the story

508
00:19:05,440 --> 00:19:08,520
seems to be that it lacks potential competition.

509
00:19:08,520 --> 00:19:12,080
So, how can we actually model potential competition?

510
00:19:12,080 --> 00:19:13,920
Well, there's a number of different ways of doing it,

511
00:19:13,920 --> 00:19:17,480
but one of the most influential in economic theory

512
00:19:17,480 --> 00:19:21,320
is the model of contestability,

513
00:19:21,320 --> 00:19:24,320
or sometimes called the contestable markets model.

514
00:19:24,320 --> 00:19:26,280
This model became popular in the late 70s,

515
00:19:26,280 --> 00:19:28,880
around the time of airline deregulation,

516
00:19:28,880 --> 00:19:32,080
because the idea was that even if there's only one airline

517
00:19:32,080 --> 00:19:32,960
that's servicing a city,

518
00:19:32,960 --> 00:19:35,720
still, you can fly planes around the country

519
00:19:35,720 --> 00:19:37,120
pretty easily, right?

520
00:19:37,120 --> 00:19:40,760
So, if, say, the only airline that flies people

521
00:19:40,760 --> 00:19:42,280
from Buffalo to Indianapolis

522
00:19:42,280 --> 00:19:45,120
is charging prices above marginal cost,

523
00:19:45,120 --> 00:19:47,280
some other airline could go and enter,

524
00:19:47,280 --> 00:19:49,320
and therefore, the firm that is already there

525
00:19:49,320 --> 00:19:52,800
needs to worry about that when it's picking its price.

526
00:19:52,800 --> 00:19:55,200
All right, so anyway, these contestability models

527
00:19:55,200 --> 00:19:57,000
are one of the main ways economists think

528
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,080
about potential competition, right?

529
00:19:59,080 --> 00:20:09,080
And the basics...


